WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.140
Film Courage: Is there an antagonist or a
dark side to romantic comedies?

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:09.740
Pamela Jaye Smith, Author: An antagonist or
a dark side?

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:21.320
I think in romantic comedies, the antagonist
is either within or usually just without,

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.400
but not big.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:29.840
Okay, take three levels of opposition: there
is internal, and then there is those around

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.600
you (family, society, culture), and then on
bigger levels of stories: there’s opposition

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:43.450
from the big outside, you know bureaucracies,
aliens, tsunamis.

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:49.190
But in a romantic comedy you want an antagonist
in opposition just at those two levels (the

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:50.790
close to levels).

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.270
This is a small story.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.140
We’re not taking on the world.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:58.080
We’re not trying to throw kingdoms.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:05.539
So your opposition can be another person or
a bunch of people (family, society, culture,

00:01:05.539 --> 00:01:11.740
sometimes religion but usually not because
there can be problems in them trying to have

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:12.740
a comedy.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.599
Trying to get into those deeper philosophical
questions .

00:01:16.599 --> 00:01:22.619
But so often, the main antagonist is within
one’s own self.

00:01:22.619 --> 00:01:25.990
Take for instance MY BEST FRIENDS’S WEDDING.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:32.099
Julia Robert’s main antagonist was herself
and what she thought she should have and how

00:01:32.099 --> 00:01:35.079
she thought it should be.

00:01:35.079 --> 00:01:39.520
And that was enough opposition to carry that
whole film because there was no one else against

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:40.520
her.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:45.539
Now the situation was against her because
the guy was engaged and he was getting married.

00:01:45.539 --> 00:01:49.360
But nobody was coming against her in particular.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.409
She was doing it all to herself.

00:01:52.409 --> 00:01:57.049
In MY BIG FAT GREEK WEDDING, you had both
those levels.

00:01:57.049 --> 00:02:01.799
The insecurity and the family saying “No!

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:06.139
You’ve got to marry a nice Greek boy and
be Greek-like…you’re a Greek person.

00:02:06.139 --> 00:02:07.139
Be a Greek person!”

00:02:07.139 --> 00:02:13.799
So two levels for antagonist in opposition
in a romantic comedy.

00:02:13.799 --> 00:02:18.870
Film Courage: I know you said earlier that
before starting this book, you weren’t a

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:24.439
huge fan of romantic comedies but what film
did you actually enjoy of this genre?

00:02:24.439 --> 00:02:31.609
Pamela Jaye Smith: Well…I did have my favorites
already and certainly LOVE ACTUALLY is my

00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:37.290
favorite because it’s got so many different
love stories in it of so many different types

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.340
and it’s so well-crafted.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:47.750
Every writer should watch and deconstruct
LOVE ACTUALLY to see how you can create and

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:56.790
sustain character arcs (many of them) over
the course of a film and have what…7, 8,

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.069
10 storylines going on?

00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:03.609
And they all work together, they are all interconnected.

00:03:03.609 --> 00:03:08.080
And it’s brilliant, it’s brilliant writing
and filmmaking.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.069
And the stories and the characters are so
endearing.

00:03:12.069 --> 00:03:19.150
So LOVE ACTUALLY…I do like DOWN WITH LOVE
and MAMMA MIA because it’s so much fun to

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.150
sing to!

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.799
Seriously, can you watch MAMMA MIA and not
sing?

00:03:23.799 --> 00:03:25.019
No you can’t!

